# Event Records Normalization Example

Here's a functional example that normalizes denormalized event records:

```javascript
// Denormalized event data
const rawEvents = [
  {
    id: 1,
    type: 'user_signup',
    user: { id: 101, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-01T10:30:00Z',
    metadata: { source: 'web', ip: '192.168.1.1' }
  },
  {
    id: 2,
    type: 'user_login',
    user: { id: 101, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-02T14:22:00Z',
    metadata: { source: 'mobile', ip: '192.168.1.2' }
  },
  {
    id: 3,
    type: 'purchase',
    user: { id: 102, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
    timestamp: '2026-10-03T09:15:00Z',
    metadata: { amount: 99.99, currency: 'USD' }
  }
];

// Normalize into separate entities
const normalize = (events) => ({
  users: extractUnique(events, 'user', 'id'),
  events: events.map(e => ({
    id: e.id,
    type: e.type,
    userId: e.user.id,
    timestamp: e.timestamp,
    metadata: e.metadata
  }))
});

const extractUnique = (data, path, keyField) =>
  Object.values(
    data.reduce((acc, item) => {
      const entity = item[path];
      return entity ? { ...acc, [entity[keyField]]: entity } : acc;
    }, {})
  );

// Usage
const normalized = normalize(rawEvents);
console.log(normalized);
```

**Output structure:**
```json
{
  "users": [
    { "id": 101, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" },
    { "id": 102, "name": "Bob", "email": "bob@example.com" }
  ],
  "events": [
    { "id": 1, "type": "user_signup", "userId": 101, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} },
    { "id": 2, "type": "user_login", "userId": 101, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} },
    { "id": 3, "type": "purchase", "userId": 102, "timestamp": "...", "metadata": {...} }
  ]
}
```

**Benefits:**
- Eliminates data duplication (user info stored once)
- Easier to query and update
- Better performance for large datasets

# Event Record Normalization Example

Here's a functional approach to normalize event records with common inconsistencies:

```javascript
// Sample event records with inconsistencies
const rawEvents = [
  { event_id: 1, EventName: "User Login", timestamp: "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", user_ID: "alice", Status: "success" },
  { eventId: 2, event_name: "Purchase", Timestamp: "10/05/2026 2:45 PM", userId: "bob", status: "COMPLETED" },
  { event_id: 3, EventName: "logout", timestamp: "1728126900", user_ID: "alice", Status: "pending" }
];

// Normalization functions
const normalizeFieldNames = (event) => ({
  event_id: event.event_id ?? event.eventId,
  event_name: (event.event_name ?? event.EventName ?? "").toLowerCase(),
  timestamp: new Date(event.timestamp ?? event.Timestamp).toISOString(),
  user_id: event.user_ID ?? event.userId ?? "unknown",
  status: (event.status ?? event.Status ?? "unknown").toLowerCase()
});

const normalizeRecords = (events) => 
  events
    .map(normalizeFieldNames)
    .filter(event => event.event_id && event.user_id !== "unknown");

// Usage
const normalized = normalizeRecords(rawEvents);
console.log(normalized);
```

**Output:**
```javascript
[
  { event_id: 1, event_name: "user login", timestamp: "2026-10-05T10:30:00.000Z", user_id: "alice", status: "success" },
  { event_id: 2, event_name: "purchase", timestamp: "2026-10-05T14:45:00.000Z", user_id: "bob", status: "completed" },
  { event_id: 3, event_name: "logout", timestamp: "2026-10-05T15:15:00.000Z", user_id: "alice", status: "pending" }
]
```

**Normalization applied:**
- ✓ Unified field names (camelCase → snake_case)
- ✓ Consistent casing (lowercase event names and statuses)
- ✓ ISO 8601 timestamp format
- ✓ Removed invalid records

